Z||000000||beachbomb||11-13-2000||04:54 PM||||Okay, heres my problem, i have an '84 Indy(4 cyclinder of course) the check engine light just came on. Does anyone know the different things that makes this light come on. Car still runs fine. plmk, thanks..jim <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fformula88||11-13-2000||05:06 PM||||You can find out exactly what its trying to tell you! Its really easy to read the codes yourself. <P>1.  Just remove the black panel thats on the front of the middle armrest (it surrounds the lighter).  Behind it is an electrical connector.  <P>2.  Jump the bottom two holes of the connector on the passenger side end.  (anyway, I think those are the correct two.  Someone confirm this)<P>3.  Turn the key to on but don't start the car.  The CEL will then flash a series of numbers.  First it will flash once, pause, then twice and pause.  Thats 12 and means its in diagnostic mode.  It will flash this code before going on.  <P>4.  Next, write down the codes it flashes.  Each will be two digit numbers and will flash like the 12 with the first number (ex. 3) then the second (2) for code 32.  ewach code will flash 3 times.  Once it is done it will start over again at 12.<P>5.  If you have a Fiero repair manual, just look up what each code is.  If not, post the codes here and myself, or someone else will gladly give you the code meanings!<P>Good luck!<P><p>[This message has been edited by Fformula88 (edited 11-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||FieroJoe||11-13-2000||05:09 PM||||A good list can be found either in haynes book or the helms manual, also here at the Fiero Online Service Guide<BR><A HREF="http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/codes.html" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nbci.com/fierov6/OSG/codes.html</A> <P>------------------<BR>"The fish who keeps on swimming is the first to chill upstream"<p>[This message has been edited by FieroJoe (edited 11-13-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||beachbomb||11-13-2000||05:56 PM||||Thanks guy! I'll check it out and luk what it is, thanks again..jim <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000004||beachbomb||11-13-2000||06:20 PM||||Well i got the code, 24, which per the web site above states it is a vehicle speed sensor, but to disregard this if vehicle is not moving, which it wasn't. So now what, this is what i saw today, i drove it home from work(40 miles-no light), park it in my driveway(running) to check the mail, get back in and the CEL is on. park the car turn it off, then start it about 30mins later to go to the store, no light. Was it just a fluck!..help, thanks..jim||||reg||5
Z||000005||Fformula88||11-13-2000||07:16 PM||||Since the light came back, it must not be just a fluke.  I'd start by disconnecting the battery for a minute or two to reset the computer.  This will erase the code and reset the computer.  Then just drive as normal.  If there is something wrong, the light will come back.  Pay close attention to when it comes on.  It should go on when the wheels are in motion, as the website said.  My Haynes manual aslo says to check the TPS sensor (Throttle Position Sensor) and the wiring connections ECM.  <P>If none of this helps, I am not sure what would be causing it.  Now that I think about it a little more, you may want to go ahead and make sure the wiring is ok bafter you disconnect the battery.  Especially around the VSS (vehicle speed sensor).  If its not making a good connection at the plug, or the insulation around the wiring is cracked it may be causing enough change in current to set the CEL off.  It would be worth a look anyway.<P>Oh, and thanks for the link FieroJoe||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||11-13-2000||07:21 PM||||The VSS won't set a code unless something is wrong with it.<P>Is the speedometer working right? If it is the problem isn't at the tranny. It's either the buffer, the wire from the buffer to the ECM, or the ECM.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Rodrv6||11-13-2000||08:39 PM||||I've had the same problem occur over the years.  Check engine light on when the car is stoppped and setting a code 24.  Occassionaly I would alse get a code 22, throttle position sensor.  In every case a new throttle position sensor would cure the code 24.  I seem to average about 50-60,000 miles between TPS changes--I've gone through about 5 or 6 over the years.  They seem to start going bad just enough to trip a code 24 long before they get bad enough to set a 22. The drivability chart in the shop manual also implicates a faulty MAP sensor. <P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting :)||||reg||4
Z||000008||beachbomb||11-13-2000||09:58 PM||||Thanks for all the advice, speedometer works fine. I'll keep an eye out to see if the CEL comes back on and luk, thanks again..jim||||reg||5
Z||000009||Indiana_resto_guy||11-13-2000||10:11 PM||||Go ahead and clear the code from the computer and see if it will come back. If the ECM has already set a code it WILL CONTINUE TO INDICATE IT untill you clear the codes, even if the problem is fixed.||||reg||1
